Id: no-sibling-plugin-reference
Pattern: (Jellyfin\.Plugin\.Requests|jellyfin-plugin-requests)
Subject: *.cs *.csproj *.props *.sln *.json
Except: none
Issue: 95

This plugin is complete with nothing behind the seam. A want goes to the local
sink whether or not a requests plugin is installed, and that is what keeps
either plugin installable alone.

The failure is gradual rather than sudden, which is why it gets a rule rather
than a paragraph. A shared type is added because both sides needed the same
shape, then a helper because it was already there, then a version constraint so
the helper keeps working, and at the end neither plugin installs without the
other and the seam is decoration. Every step in that sequence is reasonable
where it stands. The first one is the only one that is cheap to refuse.

The name the pattern matches is read from the sibling rather than guessed:

    gh api "repos/Flowfin/jellyfin-plugin-requests/git/trees/HEAD?recursive=1" --jq '.tree[].path'
    Jellyfin.Plugin.Requests/Jellyfin.Plugin.Requests.csproj
    Jellyfin.Plugin.Requests.sln

so the assembly, the namespace and the package all carry one string, and the
repository name is the second spelling because a project reference across a
checkout is written as a path rather than as an assembly name.

The subject is the source, the project and solution files, and the lock files.
The lock files are in it because a package reference reaches the resolved graph
whether or not anybody reads the project file it came from, so the closure this
build restores is judged as well as the file that asked for it.

Nothing is excepted. The interface the sibling implements is this plugin's own
and lives in this tree, so declaring it and collecting implementations of it from
the server's container costs no reference in either direction.

What it cannot do. It reads tracked text, so a workflow that fetches the sibling
at run time is outside the subject, and an assembly loaded by name at run time is
outside what any pattern here can see. It also judges this tree rather than what
ships: whether the built package's dependency list names a sibling is a property
of the artefact, and #102 is where that and the reverse direction are held.
